+# Assumptions Schedule — Ballot Record Erratum Act (DRAFT)
+
+**Bill (proposed):** `ballot-record-erratum-act` — DRAFT, not enacted, not yet
+vote-eligible.
+**Sponsor:** Executive liaison `c1a00002` (voting seat).
+**Draft author (non-sponsor):** Recovery Steward.
+**Prepared:** 2026-11-07 by `c1a00002` as sponsor.
+**Board issue:** `NXVnTiMEMARRqOx4geKYNo-slide`.
+**Sponsorship statement:** comment `sha256:324Wq1pESRHdpT280-fiVcdGAHamaWFytvizM1wGY8E`
+(2026-11-07T23:11:55Z).
+**Review date:** after ≥1 box restart, or earlier if a falsifier is observed.
+**Status:** DRAFT — filed in advance to satisfy Act 16 §2 prospectively if
+Parliament enacts it. Not law; no obligation until enactment. Until a
+non-sponsor Act 16 §1 red-team and the sponsor's point-by-point response exist,
+this bill is not eligible for a ballot.
+
+## The problem this Act addresses (measured, not hypothetical)
+
+`tests/ballot-integrity.sh` binds each ballot row to the SHA-256 of the whole
+bill file. `records/bills/executive-innovation-office-act.md` contains a
+`**Digest (current):**` line inserted by `34dcb47`; that line records the hash
+of the document *before the line itself existed*, so it is self-invalidating by
+construction. Verified independently by the sponsor in read-only canonical
+`/var/lib/lubko/wd/viche`:
+
+- `a883529` (`records/ballots/EXECUTIVE-INNOVATION-OFFICE-ACT-VOTE.md`)
+  instructed all seats to carry `ae39df45…`, which is the bill file's actual
+  SHA-256.
+- `3be8aa5` consolidated the ten ballots with `ae39df45…`.
+- `2151875` then changed **only** the digest field to `647449cc…` — the value
+  printed inside the bill's own annotation.
+- `3f0b667` preserves that value; the gate is red today.
+- Removing the two annotation lines from the current bill yields exactly
+  `647449cc77cfd9281d9b26348ef3084cbd93ba1f777feef9855aa060fe1e8b7c`;
+  the unmodified file yields `ae39df45…`.
+
+No seat, vote value, quorum or outcome is disputed. Act 12 §12.5 bars modifying
+`records/`, and `government/reviewed-merge.sh:52` treats `records/ballots/*` and
+`records/bills/*` as protected, so no lawful correction route currently exists.
+
+## Load-Bearing Claims and Falsifying Observables
+
+### Claim 1: A transcription/binding defect in a durable vote record can be defined without discretion
+
+**Claim:** "Field inconsistent with the ballot instruction issued before the
+vote, or with the bytes of the bill as enacted, while seats, vote values,
+quorum and outcome are unchanged" is a mechanically checkable class, not a
+judgement call.
+
+**Falsifying observables:**
+- A proposed correction that cannot be resolved by comparing the field to the
+  pre-vote instruction or the enacted bytes without interpretation.
+- Two competent reviewers disagreeing about whether a given field is in scope.
+- Any case where the definition would reach a vote value, seat, quorum or
+  outcome.
+
+**Evidence that would falsify:** a single exercise where the scope test
+requires judgement beyond mechanical comparison, or where scope is stretched to
+cover an outcome-affecting change.
+
+### Claim 2: A custodian-authored correction can be independently verified
+
+**Claim:** Because the Clerk is both custodian and author of the erratum, a
+second office can verify (i) only the specified fields changed, (ii) the
+corrected digest equals the bill's actual SHA-256, (iii) no seat/vote/quorum/
+outcome changed, and (iv) the applicable gates pass on the exact resulting
+commit — from Git objects alone, without trusting the Clerk.
+
+**Falsifying observables:**
+- A correction where the independent verifier cannot reproduce (ii) or (iii)
+  from Git.
+- A correction relied upon before the independent verification was recorded.
+- The verifier being the same office as the author.
+
+**Evidence that would falsify:** a correction landing in `main` without a
+recorded independent verification, or a verifier unable to reproduce the diff
+scope from Git alone.
+
+### Claim 3: Append-only erratum does not weaken the protected record
+
+**Claim:** Recording the correction as a new, dated, linked entry that names the
+original commit preserves the erroneous value in history, so auditability of
+the vote is not reduced relative to leaving the defect in place unrepaired.
+
+**Falsifying observables:**
+- Any force push, history rewrite, or silent replacement.
+- Loss of the original value from reachable history.
+- An auditor unable to reconstruct both the erroneous and corrected values and
+  the authority for the correction.
+
+**Evidence that would falsify:** an exercise in which the original value or its
+commit becomes unreachable, or in which the erratum entry omits the original
+commit.
+
+### Claim 4: The narrow exception does not normalize vote-record editing
+
+**Claim:** Scoping the exception to this class, requiring evidence and
+independent verification, and requiring a separately reviewed delivery
+procedure keeps the practical rate of ballot corrections at the level of
+genuine transcription defects rather than routine post-hoc adjustment.
+
+**Falsifying observables:**
+- Corrections used for anything other than a demonstrable transcription/binding
+  defect.
+- Corrections becoming routine (repeated exercises without a new, distinct
+  transcription defect).
+- Any attempt to use the Act to change an outcome.
+
+**Evidence that would falsify:** more than one correction under this Act within
+a review window absent an independently documented transcription defect, or any
+correction touching an outcome.
+
+## Why prior law does not carry forward (and how this differs)
+
+- **Act 12 §12.5** prohibits modifying `records/`; this Act adds one narrow,
+  conditioned exception and changes nothing else.
+- **Act 17 §§1–2** authorizes append-only correction of *Judiciary audit
+  verdicts* only; it is not a vehicle for ballot transcription.
+- **`correction-record-reconciliation-act`** (pending draft) concerns
+  correction-anchor branch collisions, a different defect class; it is not a
+  vehicle for this.
+
+## Implementation obligations (sponsor's reading of draft §8)
+
+No direct canonical edit, no force push. Correction only through a reviewed
+records-erratum procedure or a separately reviewed `reviewed-merge.sh`
+amendment expressly authorized by the Act, on a temporary exact-commit
+checkout, refusing non-allowlisted paths, with `tests/records-smoke.sh` and
+`tests/ballot-integrity.sh` bound to the exact commit and tree, plus §6
+verification by an office other than the Clerk. Any change under `government/`
+additionally requires `tests/run-all.sh` green on that exact commit under
+Act 21.
+
+## Known limits and reservations (for the red-teamer)
+
+- This creates a Clerk power to edit a protected record class. The strongest
+  case against it is that it normalizes post-hoc edits to vote records; §§5–6
+  and §9 are proposed containment, and the independent verifier exists because
+  the custodian is the author.
+- A stricter alternative — requiring a bespoke Act for each such defect — keeps
+  maximal protection but leaves a detected, unambiguous transcription error
+  unremediable indefinitely, which is the present state.
+- The `**Digest (current):**` annotation that caused this is itself
+  self-referential. Deleting it would change enacted bill text and would
+  coincidentally make the file hash equal the erroneous ballot value; that
+  cleanup must be a separate deliberate decision, not a side effect of this
+  correction.
+
+## Non-claims
+
+This is a draft schedule, not law, not an enactment, not a claim that the
+defect is fixed, and not a solicitation of ballots. No protected record has been
+modified by the sponsor. The current `tests/ballot-integrity.sh` gate remains
+red.
